<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Canton of Basel</h1><p class="st-meta">1386 CE – 1798 CE (approx.) · capital Basel</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Rhine-knee city of the Council of Basel and the humanist printers, which joined the Swiss Confederacy in 1501.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Basel was ruled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-basel">Prince-Bishopric of Basel</a>, but from the late 13th century the city council steadily acquired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s rights, buying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleinbasel">Kleinbasel</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 1392, and by the end of the 14th century it was in effect a free city of the empire. It allied with the Confederates and in 1501 joined them as the eleventh canton.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> dominated governed the city and its rural territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> met there from 1431 to 1449, and the university, the oldest in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>, was founded in 1460. Printers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-froben">Johann Froben</a> made Basel a centre of humanism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> lived there. The city adopted the Reformed faith in 1529, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> ribbons, trade and banking later enriched it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> oligarchy gave way in 1798 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>. Restored in 1803, the canton split in 1833 after civil conflict into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-stadt">Basel-Stadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basel-landschaft">Basel-Landschaft</a>, which remain separate half-cantons.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/canton-of-basel.jpg" alt="Canton of Basel" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Edi Hauri · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Canton_of_Basel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1386 CE – 1798 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Basel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Reformed Protestantism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swiss German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Switzerland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Basel, Free City of Basel</dd></dl></aside></div>
